ing result, frame removal, environment, stack, scopes, heap, return,\n   238\t  exception, and exit-code state.\n   239\t- A3: binding, iteration, branch evaluation, exact continuation, and return/pop\n   240\t  control are included.  The continuation mutation is outside the bridge match,\n   241\t  and fixed/extended complete states agree.\n   242\t- A4: all total functions have disjoint/exhaustive constructors or guards and\n   243\t  strict recursive descent.  The guarded simplification equation is true on\n   244\t  its full domain.\n   245\t- A5: `()` is a realizable input.  Mutating its true result to false is rejected\n   246\t  with a stuck `true` residual.  The opposite false-to-true bridge probe is also\n   247\t  rejected.\n   248\t\n   249\t### Gate B — PASS\n   250\t\n   251\t- B1: the formal domain is exactly strings containing only `(` and `)`, as\n   252\t  stated by the prompt.  There is no hidden length or balance restriction.\n   253\t- B2: these characters are ASCII, so the supplied semantics' ASCII string model\n   254\t  agrees with the relevant Python behavior.  K integers and Python integers are\n   255\t  unbounded for all arithmetic used here.  No exceptional operation occurs in\n   256\t  the formal domain.\n   257\t- B3: the summary recurrence directly establishes the prefix-balance\n   258\t  characterization of correct bracketing; it is not merely an opaque execution\n   259\t  summary.\n   260\t- B4: the implementation follows that characterization and all prompt examples\n   261\t  execute as specified.\n   262\t\n   263\t### Gate C — PASS\n   264\t\n   265\t- C1: the trust ledger below names all components outside the theorem.\n   266\t- C2: every claimed proof, mutation, concrete comparison, and differential test\n   267\t  has an artifact and exact command in `prove.sh`.\n   268\t- C3: formal results, finite evidence, trust, and exclusions are separated\n   269\t  here.  Finite tests are not presented as universal proofs.\n   270\t